  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 337 PM CDT Wed Jun 17 2026\n\nRelief from the heat is in sight as a cold front should drop down\ninto west Texas on Thursday evening through Friday. A shortwave \nimpulse embedded in northwest flow aloft will also traverse the \nsouthern Plains. Given the humid airmass, surface convergence, and\nupper-level support, this should generate thunderstorm chances \nstarting Thursday night and continuing Friday morning, especially \nacross the Big Country. Towards evening, the front and dryline \ncould interact with plentiful moisture. This would help new storms\nto develop across the Concho Valley, Edwards Plateau and Hill \nCountry and introduce a threat for locally heavy rainfall.\n\nChances for widespread rain should decrease on Saturday and Sunday \nas the initial upper-level wave moves eastward and the surface front \nlifts back to the north. However, a warm, humid, and somewhat \nuncapped airmass should remain in place under northwest flow aloft. \nChances for isolated to widely scattered thunderstorms will \ncontinue each afternoon, mainly across the northwest Hill Country \nwhere another shortwave trough will move out of Mexico and into \ncentral Texas. The pattern looks to remain hot with temperatures \napproaching triple digits again during the first half of next \nweek. There will continue to be some chances for evening and \novernight thunderstorms, mainly across the Big Country as models \nshow another cold front dropping into the Southern Plains.",
